/-!
# Regularity of an immersion is not inherited by the middle of a factorization

Let `k` be a field and consider the closed immersions
`Z = Spec(k[x]/(x)) --i--→ Y = Spec(k[x]/(x²)) --j--→ X = 𝐀¹_k = Spec(k[x])`.
The ideal of `Y` in `X` is `(x²)` and the ideal of `Z` in `X` is `(x)`; both are principal,
generated by a nonzerodivisor of the domain `k[x]`, so `j` and `j ∘ i` are regular immersions.
The ideal of `Z` in `Y` is `(x̄) ⊆ k[x]/(x²)`, and *every* element of that ideal kills `x̄ ≠ 0`,
so no generating family of it begins with a nonzerodivisor. Hence `i` is not a regular immersion
in any neighbourhood of the unique point of `Z`; and since `j ∘ i` *is* one, the conormal sequence
of the pair is exact and split in no neighbourhood of that point either. That is the content of
Stacks tag 0691: parts (1), (2) and (3) of Stacks tag 0690 all fail here, while the conjunction
"`j ∘ i` and `j` are regular immersions" holds, so the three parts are not equivalent to it.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_doubleOriginLineImmersion`: **`j : Y ⟶ X` is a regular
  immersion.**
* `Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_simpleOriginImmersion_comp`: **`j ∘ i : Z ⟶ X` is a regular
  immersion.**
* `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularImmersion_simpleOriginImmersion`: **`i : Z ⟶ Y` is not a regular
  immersion**, that is,
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.IsRegularImmersion` fails for it.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularImmersionAt_simpleOriginImmersion`: **`i : Z ⟶ Y` is a regular
  immersion in no neighbourhood of any point of `Z`**, which is what parts (1) and (2) of Stacks
  tag 0690 assert of `i`.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_isSplitExactConormalSequence_simpleOriginImmersion`: **the conormal
  sequence of the pair `i`, `j` is exact and split in no neighbourhood of any point of `Z`**,
  which is the second half of part (3) of Stacks tag 0690.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularImmersionAt_both_factors`,
  `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularImmersionAt_factor_and_composite` and
  `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularImmersionAt_composite_and_isSplitExactConormalSequence`: **parts
  (1), (2) and (3) of Stacks tag 0690 each fail**, at every point of `Z`.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularIdealSheaf_definingIdealOfHom_simpleOriginImmersion`: the defining
  ideal sheaf of `i : Z ⟶ Y` is not a regular ideal sheaf on `Y`.
* `Stacks.Examples.exists_isWeaklyRegular_generators_doubleOriginIdeal`: the ideal `(x²)` of `Y`
  in `X` is generated by a weakly regular sequence.
* `Stacks.Examples.exists_isWeaklyRegular_generators_simpleOriginIdeal`: the ideal `(x)` of `Z`
  in `X` is generated by a weakly regular sequence.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_exists_isWeaklyRegular_generators_doubleOriginPointIdeal`: the ideal `(x̄)`
  of `Z` in `Y` is generated by no weakly regular sequence.
* `Stacks.Examples.regular_immersion_not_permanent_in_middle`: **Stacks tag 0691**, all of these
  statements together.

## Implementation notes

All three immersions are formalized at the level of schemes. `X`, `Y` and `Z` are the spectra of
`k[x]`, `k[x]/(x²)` and `k[x]/(x)`; `j` and `j ∘ i` are `Spec` of the two quotient maps out of
`k[x]`, and `i` is `Spec` of `Stacks.Examples.doubleOriginQuotientHom`, the factorization
`k[x]/(x²) → k[x]/(x)`. All three are closed immersions, those maps being surjective, and
`Stacks.Examples.simpleOriginImmersion_comp_doubleOriginLineImmersion` identifies the composite
`i ≫ j` with `Spec` of the quotient map `k[x] → k[x]/(x)`.

The predicate is `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.IsRegularImmersion`, part (1) of
Stacks tag 063J, which is the one the statements carrying Stacks tag 0690 are about.

Stacks tag 0690 speaks of regularity *in a neighbourhood of a point*, which is
`Stacks.Examples.IsRegularImmersionAt`: regularity of the restriction of the immersion to some
open neighbourhood of that point. It follows from regularity of the immersion itself, the
inclusion of `⊤` being an isomorphism, which settles the two positive halves pointwise. For the
negative half the pointwise statement is the stronger one, and it is reached from the global one
because `Z = Spec(k)` is a one-point space, so the only open neighbourhood of its point is `⊤`.

Parts (1) and (2) of Stacks tag 0690 both have "`i` is a regular immersion in a neighbourhood of
`z`" as their first half, so the refutation of that one statement refutes both. Part (3) does not
mention `i`: it asks that `j ∘ i` be a regular immersion near `z` — which here is **true** — and
that the conormal sequence `i^* 𝒞_{Y/X} ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X} ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/Y} ⟶ 0` of Stacks tag 062S be exact and
split near `z`. So part (3) has to be refuted through that second half, and it is:
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.exists_opens_isRegularImmersion_of_split_near` — the
implication `(3) ⇒ (1)` of Stacks tag 0690, proved in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.RegularImmersion.NoetherianSplitConormal` — turns a
splitting near a point into regularity of `i` near that point, which this example refutes. Its
locally Noetherian hypothesis on `X` is met because `k[x]` is a Noetherian ring. The splitting
condition is spelled, as there, by `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.IsSplitExactConormalSequence` for the
pair `W.ι ≫ i`, `j` on an open neighbourhood `W` of the point, the two arrows being
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.conormalSequenceLeftMap` and
`Stacks.Immersion.conormalSequenceRightMap`.

Six general statements are proved here, none of which mentions the example and all of which
belong in the development proper rather than in an example file. Two are plumbing:
`Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_congr` transports regularity along an equality of morphisms,
which `rw` cannot do because the predicate carries an `IsImmersion` instance for the morphism it
is applied to, and `Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_of_comp_iso` is the converse of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularImmersion_comp_iso`. The other four are:

* `Stacks.Examples.ker_appTop_spec_map` computes `ker ((Spec φ).appTop)` as the preimage of
  `ker φ` along `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.ΓSpecIso R : Γ(Spec R, ⊤) ≅ R`; this is the naturality
  square `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.ΓSpecIso_naturality` together with the injectivity of the
  identification on the target.
* `Stacks.Examples.isRegularIdealSheaf_definingIdealOfHom_of_isRegularImmersion` reads regularity
  of a *closed* immersion `i` on the target rather than on `i.coborderRange`. It is the converse
  of `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularImmersion_of_isRegularIdealSheaf` and comes,
  like it, from the two directions of
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularIdealSheaf_comp_open_iff`.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularIdealSheaf_spec_map_of_subsingleton` refutes regularity of the
  defining ideal sheaf of `Spec φ` for a surjection `φ : R → S` with `Spec S` nonempty and
  `Spec R` a one-point space, given that `ker φ` is generated by no weakly regular list. Stacks
  tag 063G, formalized as
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isRegularIdealSheaf_iff_exists_affine_generators`,
  turns regularity into a weakly regular generating family on an affine open around each point of
  the support of the quotient; the image of a point of `Spec S` lies in that support because it
  lies in the range of a closed immersion, every open containing it is `⊤` because `Spec R` has
  one point, and `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ker_app_eq_span_range` identifies the ideal such a
  family generates over `⊤` with `ker ((Spec φ).appTop)`. Transporting along `ΓSpecIso R` — flat,
  being bijective, so weak regularity survives — produces exactly the forbidden list in `R`.
* `Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_spec_map_of_isWeaklyRegular` is the positive counterpart,
  through the same affine criterion read in the other direction: if `ker φ` is generated by a
  family that is weakly regular in `R`, then `Spec φ` is a regular immersion. One affine open,
  `⊤`, serves for every point of the support; the family is carried into `Γ(Spec R, ⊤)` along the
  inverse of `ΓSpecIso R`, and
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isGeneratedByOn_definingIdealOfHom_of_ker_app_eq_span` turns the
  resulting equality of ideals of global sections into generation of the defining ideal sheaf.

That `Spec(k[x]/(x²))` is a one-point space is proved from the surjection `k[x] → k[x]/(x²)`: a
prime of `k[x]` containing `x²` contains `x`, hence contains the maximal ideal `(x)` and equals
it, so all primes of `k[x]/(x²)` have the same contraction and `PrimeSpectrum` is a subsingleton.
That `Spec(k[x]/(x))` is one is immediate: `(x)` is maximal, so the quotient is a field.

Weak regularity, not `RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular`, is the right notion: it is the one
appearing in Stacks tag 063G for part (1), and the negative statement is stronger with it, since
a regular sequence is weakly regular.

The three ideal-level statements are phrased with `Ideal.ofList`, which is the indexing
`RingTheory.Sequence.IsWeaklyRegular` itself uses, so a negative statement about lists needs no
conversion. The affine criterion of Stacks tag 063G asks instead for a `Fin`-indexed family of
sections, so the two positive scheme-level statements are read off `Fin 1` families and their
`List.ofFn`, which is the one place the two indexings meet.

Every occurrence of the coordinate is written `(X : Polynomial k)`, never a bare `X`, including
where the surrounding term already fixes the ring. `StacksDraft.Topology.Dimension`
`.CounterexampleCodimension` puts a structure named `X` in the root namespace, and it is in this
file's import closure, so a bare `X` is an ambiguous identifier rather than `Polynomial.X`.

